1. The Big Idea
I propose to develop a Rock–Paper–Scissors Roguelike Pomodoro Game that turns time management into a strategic adventure. The game merges Pomodoro work sessions (timed rounds) with roguelike progression, where each “battle” is resolved by rock–paper–scissors mechanics. Upgrades and items provide variety, with a shop after each round to boost replayability. The MVP will cover the core gameplay loop, while optional goals might connect to academic task tracking.

2. Learning Objectives
Personal Learning Goals:

Implement a complete Python roguelike game using PyGame or tcod for turn-based and real-time play

Design a compelling game loop that motivates productivity with Pomodoro mechanics

Build and balance item systems for synergy and player choices

Practice agile solo development: iterating on feedback, planning, and feature prioritization

Optionally: Explore API integration (Canvas tasks) if time permits

3. Implementation Plan
Language/Framework: Python (PyGame or tcod for graphics & UI)

Core Steps:

Program Pomodoro timer and round management

Code RPS battle mechanics and progress system

Develop item/shop logic with basic effects and synergies

Build minimal UI and add iterative polish

(Optional) Research and prototype Canvas assignment integration

4. Project Schedule
Week 1: Game architecture, Pomodoro mechanics, basic UI

Week 2: RPS battles and player progression

Week 3: Item/shop system, playtesting, balance tweaks

Week 4: Polish, optional features, final testing & documentation

5. Collaboration Plan
As a solo developer, all planning, coding, and testing will be managed by myself. I’ll use:

GitHub for version control and milestone tracking

Agile mini-sprints (weekly self-set goals, iterative improvements)

External feedback from peers/instructor to guide polish and scope, as time permits

6. Risks and Limitations
Balancing feature ambition with available time (scope creep)

Technical learning curve (UI library, API integration)

Maintaining motivation and realistic scheduling solo

Mitigation: Focus on MVP first; stretch only if ahead of schedule; seek feedback and review progress weekly

7. Additional Course Content
Python game architecture & state management

UI/graphics libraries (PyGame/tcod)

API authentication and integration (for stretch Canvas feature)

Data persistence for saving progress
